{"name":"Quit smoking / vaping","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/E-Cig%2C_Vaping%2C_Quit_Smoking_%2825588020995%29.jpg/960px-E-Cig%2C_Vaping%2C_Quit_Smoking_%2825588020995%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E-Cig,_Vaping,_Quit_Smoking_(25588020995).jpg","caption":"E-Cig, Vaping, Quit Smoking (25588020995)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":12,"casual":{"lead":["Quitting tobacco or vaping is cited as a way to avoid or reduce 'smoker's face', the pattern of wrinkles, sagging and a dull yellowish or grayish complexion seen in many long-term smokers.","Medical sources attribute these changes to reduced oxygen and nutrient supply to skin, narrowed blood vessels, oxidative stress and breakdown of collagen and elastin. The link between smoking and facial aging is documented in observational research. No trials of quitting for appearance were found, and vaping and facial aging have not been studied."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People quit through whatever route works for them, including nicotine replacement, medication or support programmes, and often mention it as part of a broader skin and anti-aging routine. Skin blood flow is the step through which any appearance change is expected."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Quitting itself carries no appearance-specific risks in the sources reviewed. Smoking is linked to many serious diseases, so quitting has health benefits well beyond looks."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/E-Cig%2C_Vaping%2C_Quit_Smoking_%2825588020995%29.jpg/960px-E-Cig%2C_Vaping%2C_Quit_Smoking_%2825588020995%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/E-Cig%2C_Vaping%2C_Quit_Smoking_%2825588020995%29.jpg